-- Background jobs: work that would freeze a stack while it runs (a shell
-- command, a program, a large or slow file, a folder on a network share),
-- handed to the engine to do on another thread while the script goes on,
-- with a message to the object that asked when it is done.
--
-- The work is the engine's (engine/src/async.cpp, and async-core.cpp for
-- the threads): the library binds to it by name. It is a library, and not
-- new functions of the engine, so that adding it breaks no script: a
-- library's handlers come last in the message path, so a script's own
-- handlers of these names come first, as they did, and no name is reserved.
-- shell(), "open process", "put URL" and the file commands are untouched
-- and block as they always have.
--
-- The handlers' names are not all lower case: lc-compile keeps such names
-- for syntax and warns of them, and the extension build turns its warnings
-- into errors. A script finds a library handler whatever the case of the
-- name it calls.

/**
Runs shell commands and programs, reads, writes and copies files and lists
folders in the background, so that a stack stays responsive while the work
is done.

Description:
A call such as <asyncShell> or <asyncReadFile> starts a job and returns at
once with the job's number. The engine does the work on another thread
while your script goes on and the user interface stays live. When the job
is done, the object whose script started it is sent the message you named,
with the job's number, what the job made, an error (empty when all went
well) and a code:

    on mouseUp
       get asyncShell("ffmpeg -i in.mov out.mp4", "convertDone")
    end mouseUp

    on convertDone pJob, pOutput, pError, pExitCode
       if pError is not empty then
          answer "Could not convert:" && pError
       else
          put pOutput into field "Log"
       end if
    end convertDone

The message arrives as one sent with `send ... in 0 seconds` would: once
the handlers that are running have finished, or while one of them waits
`with messages`. Every job that was started sends its message exactly
once, whatever went wrong, unless the object has been deleted by then.

<asyncProcessOpen> starts a program that your script talks to: its
message comes each time the program writes something, and
<asyncProcessWrite> sends it input.

<asyncCancel> stops a job, <asyncStatus> and <asyncProgress> say how far
one has got, and <asyncJobs> lists those that are running.

The engine's own <shell>, <open process>, <URL> and file commands are as
they were: they do their work before the script goes on, as they always
have. Where a script has its own handler with the name of one of this
library's, that handler is called, as before.

Jobs that are still running when the application quits are stopped,
and the programs they started are ended. Files being written with
<asyncWriteFile> or <asyncAppendFile> are finished first.

In a standalone application, include the "Background Jobs" library
(Standalone Application Settings, Inclusions).

Tags: Library
*/

library org.openxtalk.library.async

metadata version is "1.0.0"
metadata author is "OXT-Beyond"
metadata title is "Background Jobs"

use com.livecode.foreign

private foreign handler MCAsyncJobsReadFile(in pPath as String, \
		in pMessage as String) returns UInt32 binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCAsyncJobsWriteFile(in pPath as String, \
		in pData as Data, in pAppend as CBool, in pMessage as String) \
		returns UInt32 binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCAsyncJobsCopyFile(in pFrom as String, \
		in pTo as String, in pMessage as String) returns UInt32 \
		binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCAsyncJobsListFolder(in pPath as String, \
		in pRecursive as CBool, in pMessage as String) returns UInt32 \
		binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCAsyncJobsShell(in pCommand as String, \
		in pMessage as String) returns UInt32 binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCAsyncJobsProcessOpen(in pProgram as String, \
		in pArguments as any, in pMessage as String) returns UInt32 \
		binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCAsyncJobsProcessWrite(in pJob as any, \
		in pData as Data) returns CBool binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCAsyncJobsProcessCloseInput(in pJob as any) \
		returns CBool binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCAsyncJobsCancel(in pJob as any) returns CBool \
		binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCAsyncJobsStatus(in pJob as any, \
		out rStatus as String) returns nothing binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCAsyncJobsProgress(in pJob as any, \
		out rProgress as String) returns nothing binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCAsyncJobsList(out rJobs as String) \
		returns nothing binds to "<builtin>"
private foreign handler MCAsyncJobsEndWithParent(in pParent as any) \
		returns CBool binds to "<builtin>"

/**
Summary: Runs a shell command in the background and sends a message with its output when it has ended.

Example:
on mouseUp
   get asyncShell("ls -l /usr/bin", "listingDone")
end mouseUp

on listingDone pJob, pOutput, pError, pExitCode
   if pError is empty then put pOutput into field "Listing"
end listingDone

Example:
-- keep the number, to cancel the command if the user gives up
put asyncShell("rsync -a" && tFrom && tTo, "syncDone") into sSyncJob

Parameters:
pCommand: The command, as <shell> takes it.
pMessage: The name of the message to send to this object when the command has ended.

Returns: The number of the job.

Description:
Use the <asyncShell> function in place of <shell> for a command that
takes long enough to freeze the stack: a conversion, a download, a
build, a search of the disk.

The command is run exactly as <shell> runs it: by the <shellCommand>,
with its error output joined to its output, and on Windows in a console
window that the <hideConsoleWindows> property hides. <asyncShell>
returns at once. When the command has ended, the object whose script
called <asyncShell> is sent <pMessage> with four parameters:

- the number of the job
- the command's output, as text, as <shell> returns it
- an error: empty if the command ran, "cancelled" if <asyncCancel>
  stopped it, "can't run shell" if the shell could not be started
- the command's exit code (what <shell> leaves in the <result>, and 0
  for a command that succeeded). A command that a signal ended has 128
  plus the signal's number.

Output in UTF-8 is decoded as it is with <shell>: with
`textDecode(pOutput, "UTF-8")`.

To see a command's output while it runs, or to send it input, start it
with <asyncProcessOpen>.

If the <securityPermissions> do not allow processes, or there is no
<shellCommand>, the call fails with an error, as <shell> does. Where a
script has its own <asyncShell> handler, that handler is called
instead.

On Android and in a browser no command can be run: the message comes
with the error "not supported".

Tags: Library

References: shell (function), shellCommand (property),
hideConsoleWindows (property), asyncProcessOpen (handler),
asyncCancel (handler), result (function), textDecode (function)
*/
public handler asyncShell(in pCommand as String, in pMessage as String) \
		returns Number
	variable tJob as Number
	unsafe
		put MCAsyncJobsShell(pCommand, pMessage) into tJob
	end unsafe
	return tJob
end handler

/**
Summary: Starts a program in the background and sends a message each time it writes output, and when it ends.

Example:
on mouseUp
   local tArguments
   put "-c" into tArguments[1]
   put "4" into tArguments[2]
   put "openxtalk.org" into tArguments[3]
   get asyncProcessOpen("ping", tArguments, "pingSaid")
end mouseUp

on pingSaid pJob, pEvent, pData, pCode
   switch pEvent
      case "output"
         put pData after field "Log"
         break
      case "exit"
         put "Done, with exit code" && pCode & return after field "Log"
         break
      case "failed"
         answer "Could not run ping:" && pData
         break
   end switch
end pingSaid

Example:
-- a program that is sent lines to work on
put asyncProcessOpen("/usr/bin/sort", empty, "sortSaid") into sSortJob
asyncProcessWrite sSortJob, "pear" & return & "apple" & return
asyncProcessCloseInput sSortJob

Parameters:
pProgram: The program: a path, or a name alone to have the system look for it in its PATH.
pArguments: The program's arguments: an array with the keys 1 to N, or text with one argument on each line, or empty.
pMessage: The name of the message to send to this object.

Returns: The number of the job.

Description:
Use the <asyncProcessOpen> function to run a program whose output you
want as it comes, or which you send input to, without the stack waiting
for it.

The program is started directly, not by a shell: each argument reaches
it exactly as it is given, with nothing to quote. <asyncProcessOpen>
returns at once. The object whose script called it is then sent
<pMessage> with four parameters: the number of the job, an event, data
and a code. The events:

- "output": the program wrote to its output. The third parameter is
  what it wrote, as binary data (use <textDecode> for text that is not
  plain ASCII). What a program writes can arrive in pieces of any size,
  not line by line.
- "error": the same for its error output.
- "exit": the program has ended. The fourth parameter is its exit
  code (128 plus the signal's number for one that a signal ended).
- "cancelled": <asyncCancel> stopped it.
- "failed": it could not be started, or went wrong. The third
  parameter says why ("can't run program", "not supported", "out of
  memory") and the fourth is the system's error code.

Exactly one of "exit", "cancelled" and "failed" is sent, and it is the
job's last message: all the program's output has been sent before it.

Send the program input with <asyncProcessWrite>, and end its input with
<asyncProcessCloseInput>. Stop it with <asyncCancel>, which also ends
the programs that it started.

On Windows the program is started as <open process> starts one: in a
console of its own, which the <hideConsoleWindows> property hides. A
batch file (.bat or .cmd) is refused, because Windows hands its
arguments to the command interpreter, where one could become a command:
run it with <asyncShell>.

If the <securityPermissions> do not allow processes, the call fails
with an error. Where a script has its own <asyncProcessOpen> handler,
that handler is called instead.

On Android and in a browser no program can be run: the message comes
with the event "failed" and "not supported".

Tags: Library

References: open process (command), asyncProcessWrite (handler),
asyncProcessCloseInput (handler), asyncCancel (handler),
asyncShell (handler), hideConsoleWindows (property),
textDecode (function)
*/
public handler asyncProcessOpen(in pProgram as String, in pArguments as any, \
		in pMessage as String) returns Number
	variable tJob as Number
	unsafe
		put MCAsyncJobsProcessOpen(pProgram, pArguments, pMessage) into tJob
	end unsafe
	return tJob
end handler

/**
Summary: Sends input to a program started with <asyncProcessOpen>.

Example:
asyncProcessWrite sJob, "status" & return

Example:
asyncProcessWrite sJob, textEncode(field "Question", "UTF-8") & return
if the result is not empty then answer "The program has ended."

Parameters:
pJob: The number of the job, as <asyncProcessOpen> returned it.
pData: What to send: binary data, or text, which is sent as its native bytes (use <textEncode> for another encoding).

Description:
Use the <asyncProcessWrite> command to send input to a program that
<asyncProcessOpen> started. The command returns at once: what is sent
is queued and written to the program in the background, in the order
of the calls.

The <result> is empty, or "not running" if <pJob> is not a program that
is running with its input open: nothing is sent then.

Tags: Library

References: asyncProcessOpen (handler), asyncProcessCloseInput (handler),
textEncode (function), result (function)
*/
public handler asyncProcessWrite(in pJob as any, in pData as Data) \
		returns String
	variable tWritten as Boolean
	unsafe
		put MCAsyncJobsProcessWrite(pJob, pData) into tWritten
	end unsafe
	if tWritten then
		return ""
	end if
	return "not running"
end handler

/**
Summary: Ends the input of a program started with <asyncProcessOpen>.

Example:
asyncProcessWrite sJob, tLastLines
asyncProcessCloseInput sJob

Parameters:
pJob: The number of the job, as <asyncProcessOpen> returned it.

Description:
Use the <asyncProcessCloseInput> command to tell a program that there
is no more input, as the end of a file would. Many programs do their
work, or end, only then. What <asyncProcessWrite> has queued is written
first.

The <result> is empty, or "not running" if <pJob> is not a program that
is running.

Tags: Library

References: asyncProcessOpen (handler), asyncProcessWrite (handler),
result (function)
*/
public handler asyncProcessCloseInput(in pJob as any) returns String
	variable tClosed as Boolean
	unsafe
		put MCAsyncJobsProcessCloseInput(pJob) into tClosed
	end unsafe
	if tClosed then
		return ""
	end if
	return "not running"
end handler

/**
Summary: Reads a whole file in the background and sends a message with its contents.

Example:
on mouseUp
   get asyncReadFile(specialFolderPath("documents") & "/big.csv", "csvRead")
end mouseUp

on csvRead pJob, pData, pError, pCode
   if pError is not empty then
      answer "Could not read the file:" && pError
      exit csvRead
   end if
   put textDecode(pData, "UTF-8") into field "Table"
end csvRead

Parameters:
pPath: The path of the file.
pMessage: The name of the message to send to this object when the file has been read.

Returns: The number of the job.

Description:
Use the <asyncReadFile> function to read a file that is large, or is on
a slow disk or a network share, without the stack waiting for it.

<asyncReadFile> returns at once. When the file has been read, the
object whose script called it is sent <pMessage> with four parameters:

- the number of the job
- the file's contents, as binary data: what
  `URL ("binfile:" & pPath)` gives. Use <textDecode> to make text of it.
- an error: empty, or "can't open file", "error reading file",
  "file too large" (4 GB or more), "out of memory" or "cancelled"
- the system's error code, when the system reported one

A path that is not absolute is taken from the <defaultFolder> as it is
when <asyncReadFile> is called.

If the <securityPermissions> do not allow access to the disk, the call
fails with an error. Where a script has its own <asyncReadFile>
handler, that handler is called instead.

On Android, a file inside the application's package is not a file of
the file system and cannot be read this way: read it with <URL>.

Tags: Library

References: URL (keyword), asyncWriteFile (handler),
asyncProgress (handler), asyncCancel (handler), textDecode (function),
defaultFolder (property)
*/
public handler asyncReadFile(in pPath as String, in pMessage as String) \
		returns Number
	variable tJob as Number
	unsafe
		put MCAsyncJobsReadFile(pPath, pMessage) into tJob
	end unsafe
	return tJob
end handler

/**
Summary: Writes a file in the background and sends a message when it is written.

Example:
on saveDocument
   local tData
   put textEncode(field "Document", "UTF-8") into tData
   get asyncWriteFile(the cDocumentPath of this stack, tData, "documentSaved")
end saveDocument

on documentSaved pJob, pResult, pError, pCode
   if pError is not empty then answer "Could not save:" && pError
end documentSaved

Parameters:
pPath: The path of the file.
pData: What to write: binary data, or text, which is written as its native bytes (use <textEncode> for another encoding).
pMessage: The name of the message to send to this object when the file has been written.

Returns: The number of the job.

Description:
Use the <asyncWriteFile> function to write a file without the stack
waiting for the disk. The file is made if it is not there, and replaced
if it is, as `put pData into URL ("binfile:" & pPath)` does.

<asyncWriteFile> returns at once. When the file has been written, the
object whose script called it is sent <pMessage> with four parameters:
the number of the job, empty, an error (empty, or "can't open file",
"error writing file" or "cancelled") and the system's error code, when
the system reported one.

The data is as it was when <asyncWriteFile> was called: changing the
variable afterwards changes nothing of what is written.

A write that fails or is cancelled can leave part of the file. If the
application quits while a file is being written, the write is finished
first.

Writes started one after the other do not wait for each other: do not
write the same file twice at once. To add to a file, use
<asyncAppendFile>.

On macOS the file is not given the type and creator of the <fileType>
property.

If the <securityPermissions> do not allow access to the disk, the call
fails with an error. Where a script has its own <asyncWriteFile>
handler, that handler is called instead.

Tags: Library

References: URL (keyword), asyncAppendFile (handler),
asyncReadFile (handler), asyncProgress (handler), textEncode (function),
fileType (property)
*/
public handler asyncWriteFile(in pPath as String, in pData as Data, \
		in pMessage as String) returns Number
	variable tJob as Number
	unsafe
		put MCAsyncJobsWriteFile(pPath, pData, false, pMessage) into tJob
	end unsafe
	return tJob
end handler

/**
Summary: Adds to the end of a file in the background and sends a message when it is written.

Example:
get asyncAppendFile(tLogPath, the internet date & tab & tEvent & return, "logWritten")

Parameters:
pPath: The path of the file.
pData: What to add: binary data, or text, which is written as its native bytes (use <textEncode> for another encoding).
pMessage: The name of the message to send to this object when the data has been written.

Returns: The number of the job.

Description:
Use the <asyncAppendFile> function to add to a file, such as a log,
without the stack waiting for the disk. The file is made if it is not
there.

It works as <asyncWriteFile> does, and sends the same message. Appends
to one file that are started one after the other may be written in
either order, each of them whole: start the next from the message of the
one before where the order matters.

Tags: Library

References: asyncWriteFile (handler), URL (keyword)
*/
public handler asyncAppendFile(in pPath as String, in pData as Data, \
		in pMessage as String) returns Number
	variable tJob as Number
	unsafe
		put MCAsyncJobsWriteFile(pPath, pData, true, pMessage) into tJob
	end unsafe
	return tJob
end handler

/**
Summary: Copies a file in the background and sends a message when it is copied.

Example:
on backUp
   put asyncCopyFile(tVideoPath, tBackupFolder & "/video.mov", "copyDone") into sCopyJob
   send "showCopyProgress" to me in 200 milliseconds
end backUp

on showCopyProgress
   local tProgress
   put asyncProgress(sCopyJob) into tProgress
   if tProgress is empty then exit showCopyProgress
   set the endValue of scrollbar "Progress" to item 2 of tProgress
   set the thumbPosition of scrollbar "Progress" to item 1 of tProgress
   send "showCopyProgress" to me in 200 milliseconds
end showCopyProgress

Parameters:
pFrom: The path of the file to copy.
pTo: The path of the copy.
pMessage: The name of the message to send to this object when the file has been copied.

Returns: The number of the job.

Description:
Use the <asyncCopyFile> function to copy a file, however large, without
the stack waiting for it. The copy has the file's contents, permissions
and dates. A file that is at <pTo> is replaced.

<asyncCopyFile> returns at once. When the file has been copied, the
object whose script called it is sent <pMessage> with four parameters:
the number of the job, empty, an error (empty, or "can't open file",
"error reading file", "error writing file" or "cancelled") and the
system's error code, when the system reported one.

A copy that fails or is cancelled leaves no file at <pTo>. Copying a
file onto itself fails with "can't open file".

<pTo> is the path of the copy itself, not of a folder to put it in.
Folders are not copied.

If the <securityPermissions> do not allow access to the disk, the call
fails with an error. Where a script has its own <asyncCopyFile>
handler, that handler is called instead.

Tags: Library

References: asyncProgress (handler), asyncCancel (handler),
revCopyFile (command)
*/
public handler asyncCopyFile(in pFrom as String, in pTo as String, \
		in pMessage as String) returns Number
	variable tJob as Number
	unsafe
		put MCAsyncJobsCopyFile(pFrom, pTo, pMessage) into tJob
	end unsafe
	return tJob
end handler

/**
Summary: Lists a folder in the background, with the folders inside it if asked, and sends a message with the listing.

Example:
on mouseUp
   get asyncListFolder("//server/projects", true, "projectsListed")
end mouseUp

on projectsListed pJob, pListing, pError, pCode
   if pError is not empty then exit projectsListed
   -- the files only: a folder's line ends with a slash
   filter pListing without ("*" & slash)
   put pListing into field "Files"
end projectsListed

Parameters:
pPath: The path of the folder.
pRecursive: True to list the folders inside the folder too, and those inside them; false for the folder alone.
pMessage: The name of the message to send to this object when the folder has been listed.

Returns: The number of the job.

Description:
Use the <asyncListFolder> function to list a folder that is large, or
on a network share, or a whole tree of folders, without the stack
waiting for it.

<asyncListFolder> returns at once. When the folder has been listed, the
object whose script called it is sent <pMessage> with four parameters:

- the number of the job
- the listing: one line for each file and folder, with a line break
  after the last too. A line is the path from <pPath>, with "/"
  between its parts, and a folder's ends with "/". The entries of a
  folder are in the order of their bytes (not that of <files>), and a
  folder is followed by what it holds.
- an error: empty, or "can't open folder", "out of memory" or
  "cancelled"
- the system's error code, when the system reported one

A link to a folder is listed as a folder and not gone into. A folder
inside <pPath> that cannot be read is listed and left out, without an
error. A file whose name has a line break in it is left out.

If the <securityPermissions> do not allow access to the disk, the call
fails with an error. Where a script has its own <asyncListFolder>
handler, that handler is called instead.

Tags: Library

References: files (function), folders (function),
asyncProgress (handler), asyncCancel (handler), filter (command)
*/
public handler asyncListFolder(in pPath as String, in pRecursive as Boolean, \
		in pMessage as String) returns Number
	variable tJob as Number
	unsafe
		put MCAsyncJobsListFolder(pPath, pRecursive, pMessage) into tJob
	end unsafe
	return tJob
end handler

/**
Summary: Stops a background job.

Example:
on closeStack
   asyncCancel sConvertJob
end closeStack

Parameters:
pJob: The number of the job, as the function that started it returned it.

Description:
Use the <asyncCancel> command to stop a job that is no longer wanted. A
shell command or a program is ended at once, with the programs it
started; a file job stops at its next step.

The job still sends its message, once, with the error "cancelled" (for
a program started with <asyncProcessOpen>, the event "cancelled"); a
job that was over before it could be stopped sends the message it would
have sent. A cancelled shell command's message has the output it had
written.

The <result> is empty, or "no such job" if <pJob> is not a job that is
running or waiting to: one that has ended, or a variable that was never
given a job's number. That is not an error.

Tags: Library

References: asyncShell (handler), asyncProcessOpen (handler),
asyncStatus (handler), result (function)
*/
public handler asyncCancel(in pJob as any) returns String
	variable tCancelled as Boolean
	unsafe
		put MCAsyncJobsCancel(pJob) into tCancelled
	end unsafe
	if tCancelled then
		return ""
	end if
	return "no such job"
end handler

/**
Summary: Says whether a background job is waiting, running or over.

Example:
if asyncStatus(sConvertJob) is not empty then
   answer "The conversion is still running."
end if

Parameters:
pJob: The number of the job, as the function that started it returned it.

Returns: "queued" for a job that is waiting for others to end, "running" for one that is being done, and empty for one that is over (its last message has been sent, or is about to arrive) or that never was.

Description:
Use the <asyncStatus> function to find out whether a job is still to
come. Four file jobs are done at once, and the others wait their turn,
"queued"; shell commands and programs start at once.

Tags: Library

References: asyncProgress (handler), asyncJobs (handler),
asyncCancel (handler)
*/
public handler asyncStatus(in pJob as any) returns String
	variable tStatus as String
	unsafe
		MCAsyncJobsStatus(pJob, tStatus)
	end unsafe
	return tStatus
end handler

/**
Summary: Says how far a background job has got.

Example:
put asyncProgress(sCopyJob) into tProgress
if tProgress is not empty and item 2 of tProgress > 0 then
   put round(100 * item 1 of tProgress / item 2 of tProgress) & "%" into field "Percent"
end if

Parameters:
pJob: The number of the job, as the function that started it returned it.

Returns: Two numbers with a comma between them: how much has been done and how much there is to do, or empty for a job that is over or never was.

Description:
Use the <asyncProgress> function to show the progress of a job while it
runs, for example from a handler that sends itself a message every few
hundred milliseconds.

For reading, writing and copying a file the numbers are bytes. For a
folder listing the first is the number of entries found, and for a
shell command or a program it is the number of bytes of output so far;
the second is 0 for these, since the total is not known.

Tags: Library

References: asyncStatus (handler), asyncCopyFile (handler),
asyncReadFile (handler)
*/
public handler asyncProgress(in pJob as any) returns String
	variable tProgress as String
	unsafe
		MCAsyncJobsProgress(pJob, tProgress)
	end unsafe
	return tProgress
end handler

/**
Summary: Lists the background jobs that have not ended.

Example:
on closeStackRequest
   if asyncJobs() is not empty then
      answer "Work is still running. Close anyway?" with "Cancel" or "Close"
      if it is "Cancel" then exit closeStackRequest
   end if
   pass closeStackRequest
end closeStackRequest

Returns: The numbers of the jobs whose last message has not been sent, one on each line, in the order they were started. Empty if there are none.

Description:
Use the <asyncJobs> function to find out whether any background work is
still to be done, or to stop all of it:

    repeat for each line tJob in asyncJobs()
       asyncCancel tJob
    end repeat

The list has the jobs of every stack, not only those that this object
started.

Tags: Library

References: asyncStatus (handler), asyncCancel (handler)
*/
public handler asyncJobs() returns String
	variable tJobs as String
	unsafe
		MCAsyncJobsList(tJobs)
	end unsafe
	return tJobs
end handler

/**
Summary: Ends this process as soon as the process that started it ends.

Example:
-- in a helper that another program started, with its process number
get asyncEndWithParent($MY_PARENT_PROCESS)

Parameters:
pParent: The process number of the program that started this one (its <processID>, which it must pass to this one, in its environment or on the command line).

Returns: True if this process now ends with that one. False if <pParent> is not the number of the process that started this one (that process may have ended already), or where no process can be started (Android, iOS and a browser).

Description:
Use the <asyncEndWithParent> function in a program that another started
to work for it, and that is of no use once that one has gone. A program
that ends of its own accord stops those it started with
<asyncProcessOpen>, but one that crashes, or that the system or a user
kills, stops none of them, and one that is busy can run on for ever.
After <asyncEndWithParent> this process ends, at once and without
running any more of its scripts, when that one ends in any way.

The Background Workers library has each worker call it, with the number
of the stack's process, as it starts.

Tags: Library

References: asyncProcessOpen (handler), processID (function)
*/
public handler asyncEndWithParent(in pParent as any) returns Boolean
	variable tWatching as Boolean
	unsafe
		put MCAsyncJobsEndWithParent(pParent) into tWatching
	end unsafe
	return tWatching
end handler

end library
